 RSTS/E TCP/IP: an IP stack for RSTS/E 10.1 on the XE: (DEUNA/DELUA)
 Ethernet portal interface, with these commands:

   PING       test that a host answers
   HOST       look up names; HOST alone shows the settings
   IFCONFIG   the interface: addresses, DHCP lease, traffic
   CURL       fetch a web page (https:// through sslproxy)
   NC         a TCP connection on your terminal
   NETDATE    the date and time from a time server
   TLOAD      the MiSTer tape library: put a tape on MT0:
   TDIR       the MiSTer tape library: list it

 The settings are in TCPIP$:TCPIP.INI. TCPIP$:TCPIP.INS starts the
 stack (the TCPIPD and TFTPD jobs) and defines the commands; add
     $ @SY:[0,91]TCPIP.INS
 to [0,1]START.COM to have it at every boot.

 Every command reads its whole command line, up to 127 characters, and
 takes RSTS-style qualifiers (TLOAD/UNLOAD, NETDATE/SET/QUICK). DCL
 upper-cases what you type outside quotes: put anything whose case
 matters, such as a URL path, in double quotes.
*Settings
 TCPIP$:TCPIP.INI holds one setting per line; ! starts a comment.
 Changes take effect when TCP/IP next starts. The settings:

   IP        DHCP or a.b.c.d    by DHCP (default), or this address
   MASK      a.b.c.d            network mask, with a fixed address
   GATEWAY   a.b.c.d            default gateway, with a fixed address
   DNS       a.b.c.d [a.b.c.d]  one or two name servers (else from DHCP)
   DOMAIN    name               tried after short names
   HOSTNAME  name               sent to DHCP (default: DECnet name)
   DECNET    1.10 RSTS1         with DECnet/E: its node address, name
   MAC       08-00-2B-12-34-56  use this Ethernet address
   UNIT      0                  controller unit (XE0: is 0)
   DETACH    YES                run TCPIPD and TFTPD as detached jobs
   TFTP      YES                run the TFTP server (NO turns it off)
   PROXY     host:port          HTTP proxy for CURL (for https://)
   NTP       server             time server for NETDATE
   TIMEZONE  PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0   local time zone (POSIX TZ string)
*Servers
 TCPIP.INS starts two detached jobs: TCPIPD, the stack itself (ARP,
 IP, ICMP, UDP, TCP, DHCP), and TFTPD, a TFTP server for the files in
 FTP$: (a system logical for one account, for example
     $ ASSIGN/SYSTEM SY:[1,100] FTP$).
 To stop them, REMOVE their jobs; to restart, run TCPIP.INS again.
